She succumbed after 6 years. Pathological examination has
been previously described.
3
Summary
This family has autosomal dominant parkinsonism
because of a-synuclein triplication. Dopamine-responsive
parkinsonism, cognitive difﬁculties, RBD, and dysautonomia
are common. End-stage illness includes contractures, myo-
clonus, and severe motor and cognitive impairment. Of note,
there was presymptomatic amphetamine/cocaine use in 2 of
the cases described.
