induced significant reductions in the severity of ( A) global symptoms ( v2(4) 5 31.12, P < .0001), ( B) total tics ( v2(4) 5 34.16, P < .0001), ( C) motor
tics ( v2(4) 5 34.16, P < .0001), and ( D) phonic tics ( v2(4) 5 34.62, P < .0001), as assessed by Yale Global Tic Severity Scale (YGTSS) scores. Analy-
ses of Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y -BOCS) scores in the 6 patients with obsessive–compulsive symptoms revealed a significant
reduction in ( E) total scores ( Z 5 2.20, P 5 .028) and ( F) compulsion subscale scores ( Z 5 2.20, P 5 .028) but not in ( G) obsession subscale scores
(Z 5 1.83, P 5 .068, NS), *** P < .001, ** P < .01, * P < .05 versus baseline scores (after adjustment for multiple comparisons for YGTSS scores). Analy-
ses of YGTSS scores were performed with Friedman’s test, followed by the Wilcoxon-Nemenyi-McDonald-Thompson test for post hoc comparisons.
Analyses of Y -BOCS scores were performed with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test.
